@Tutorial(time: 20) {
   @Intro(title: "Testing a Sorting Algorithm") {
      Sorting is a classic property-testing target. A correct sorting function
      satisfies three independent properties that you can verify separately —
      and any one of them would catch most bugs.
   }

   @Section(title: "The Three Laws of Sorting") {
      @ContentAndMedia {
         A correct sorting function must satisfy:

         1. **Order** — the output is in non-decreasing order
         2. **Permutation** — the output contains exactly the same elements as the input
         3. **Idempotence** — sorting the output again produces the same output

         These properties are independent. A buggy sort might satisfy two of them
         while violating the third, so testing all three gives much stronger coverage.
      }
      @Steps {
         @Step {
            Start with the function under test. In a real project this would be
            your custom sort implementation; here we use a placeholder that we'll
            break later.

            @Code(name: "SortTests.swift", file: "t03-s01-setup.swift")
         }
      }
   }

   @Section(title: "Property 1: Order") {
      @ContentAndMedia {
         The simplest invariant: every adjacent pair in the sorted output must
         be in non-decreasing order. This catches the most common sort bugs.
      }
      @Steps {
         @Step {
            Write a helper that checks whether an array is sorted, then write
            the order property.

            @Code(name: "SortTests.swift", file: "t03-s02-order-property.swift")
         }
         @Step {
            The strategy generates integer arrays of length 0 to 30.
            Edge-biased length generation means the engine tries empty and
            single-element arrays frequently.

            @Code(name: "SortTests.swift", file: "t03-s03-order-with-strategy.swift")
         }
      }
   }

   @Section(title: "Property 2: Permutation") {
      @ContentAndMedia {
         Sorting must not add, remove, or duplicate elements. Compare the sorted
         multisets, not the arrays, because order differs by definition.
      }
      @Steps {
         @Step {
            Write the permutation property. Sorting the elements of both the
            input and output and comparing the frequency counts confirms that
            the same elements appear the same number of times.

            @Code(name: "SortTests.swift", file: "t03-s04-permutation-property.swift")
         }
      }
   }

   @Section(title: "Property 3: Idempotence") {
      @ContentAndMedia {
         Sorting an already-sorted array must produce the same array. This is
         trivially true for correct sorts but catches implementations that
         mutate their input or have unstable behaviour.
      }
      @Steps {
         @Step {
            Write the idempotence property.

            @Code(name: "SortTests.swift", file: "t03-s05-idempotence-property.swift")
         }
      }
   }

   @Section(title: "Introducing a Bug") {
      @ContentAndMedia {
         Replace the correct sort with a broken version that drops the largest
         element from arrays longer than 3. All three properties will catch it,
         but the counterexamples they produce are different — and each one
         explains the bug from a different angle.
      }
      @Steps {
         @Step {
            Introduce a broken sort implementation.

            @Code(name: "SortTests.swift", file: "t03-s06-broken-sort.swift")
         }
         @Step {
            Run the tests. The permutation property fails first with a 4-element
            counterexample. The order property passes (the output is still in
            order — it just has a missing element). This shows why testing
            multiple independent properties matters.

            @Code(name: "Terminal", file: "t03-s07-output.txt")
         }
      }
   }
}
